[Music]  when we talk about opioids we refer to  things like narcotics is another name  for opioids and this includes  medications like oxycodone or morphine  fentanyl there are many opioids and all  of them are used for pain and some of  them are natural occurring opioids such  as morphine and codeine and there are  some that are semi synthetic such as  hydrocodone oxycodone and hydromorphone  and then we have fully synthetic drugs  like methadone and fentanyl so all of  these drugs are used for both chronic  cancer pain and chronic non-cancer pain  as well as in some acute situations when  it's appropriate opioid use is a very  common medical treatment for pain and a  few other disorders if you need opioids  you should talk with your doctor about  that need and if it's specific to paying  for example you might ask whether there  are other pain relievers that are less  powerful that will still do the job if  you decide to undertake opioid treatment  with your physician I think it's very  important that you not exceed the  prescribed dose and the prescribed  frequency once you have taken opioids  for a while it's been important not to  stop without talking to your doctor and  developing a plan for reducing that use  pain is one of the most common things  that we treat in the emergency  department it's by far the most common  reason why patients present to the  emergency department we know that well  there's different studies but definitely  over fifty percent of patients present  because there's some sort of a pain that  could have been because they sprained  their ankle and that hurts or their  chest hurts or their abdomen hurts but  it's paint we as clinicians want to do  whatever we can to alleviate  pain this is this as part of our arm or  obligation we use medications like see  the medicine ibuprofen as best we can  but we do have this class of medications  which are the opioids even in the course  of my career probably starting around  two thousand or so there's started to be  this recognition that we were under  treating pain many studies came out that  showed that that we were allowing  patients to suffer needlessly we weren't  giving them adequate medication and this  led to lots of different standards that  happen there's an initiative that says  pain is the fifth vital sign for example  they started asking about pain treatment  on on satisfaction surveys that patients  got now they started using it as a  quality measure as well too so I think  that what happened was clinicians the  first inclination was to use the  medications that we knew worked for pain  and those are opioids they're the most  potent medications we have for pain and  so we started prescribing more and more  and I can tell you that we used to go  from one or two tylenol with codeine to  then using one or two tablets of  oxycodone to then using lots of  hydromorphone a really strong opioid and  we started using more and more at a time  to the point where the pendulum  definitely swung too far to the side of  overtreatment and now we're we're in  this phase right now it's not just the  emergency physicians it's we know that  it's it's basically all clinicians we  know that the prescribing of opioids  increased markedly probably at least  four fold if you look at the actual  numbers of milligrams probably tenfold  over the past 20 years and so now we're  in this area where we are right now I  think I think the the current strategy  that we have at least in the emergency  department is to minimize the use of  opioids as much as possible so we do try  and use see the minocin ibuprofen we use  patches that have lidocaine that help  with the symptoms we're trying to do do  better a better job with regional blocks  these are where we inject a little bit  of anesthetic around a nerve and this  can make the pain for that area  completely go away for a significant  amount of time we're also recognizing  that we still need to use opioids in  some situations but we don't need to use  so much we now know that a cute  really severe pain probably laughs only  two to three days and so there's no  point in getting a prescription with 30  pills it's prone it's probably  sufficient to have eight or ten pills  because we know that there's left over  20 pills then stay in the medicine  cabinet they get diverted they get  abused and that's where people get into  trouble so I think that there's this new  recognition about the the problem and I  think there's a new sophistication with  how a reason these medications as well  in addition to multimodal pain treatment  that's using every opportunity we have  to treat pain before we reach for the  opioid frequently in the emergency  department when someone has pain they  want to feel better and sometimes they  fit quite feeling better with the opioid  medication in this new era of  sophistication where we're trying to be  very good about how we're using opioids  we're trying to reduce their utilization  as much as possible but sometimes tricky  patients want to have the strong  medication you want a certain amount but  we know that there's harm associated  with it if a patient comes in and they  are requesting an opioid I now sit down  with the patient to have a discussion we  have good research that came out that  showed that if you have back pain and  you get an opioid your outcomes are  exactly the same at five days as if you  hadn't had the opioid so what's the  point of having the risk of that  medication we know that if you maximize  the non opioid medications ibuprofen  acetaminophen pain patches like  lidocaine patches for example then often  people will feel entirely better so why  do I take the risk of the opioid the  number that sticks in my head that I  often tell patients is fifteen percent  I've seen that over and over again in  many studies whether it be risk of my  nurse or having minor surgery getting a  first prescription for an opioid naive  patient in the emergency department aims  at about fifteen percent of them will  still be on opioids at a year even if  they're opioid naive before and that was  their very first prescription so I tell  patients about fifteen percent of  patients that I give that first  prescription to will still be dependent  on minute a year it's not worth the risk  in most cases so when treating pain  opioids are extremely powerful and there  is clearly a role for them the problem  that we have currently is that  practitioners are literally all over the  map  some people will not prescribe anything  at any for any condition and some people  will give 30 60 90 pills of oxycodone or  hydrocodone and so I think what's  happening is that we're creating a  severe so severe problem of expectations  for patients because they don't know  what they're going to get from which  doctor we need to do a better job with  that we're starting to see guidelines  that are coming up such as from the CDC  which need guidelines we need to get on  the same page even after procedures for  example after a normal childbirth or a  cesarean section we found that if  situations are extremely varied with  with the numbers of pills that they give  for patients we see that after  orthopedic surgery as well after after  general surgery like appendicitis for  example some people will give no opioids  and will give a lot of opioids and it's  that this is creating a problem so we  need some some form of standardization  obviously we need to recognize that  every patient is different and their  needs will be different but we can't  have this this tenfold difference in the  amount of pain medications that are  given to people the thing that I'm most  concerned about no it's not just it's  not the treatment of acute pain it's  what's left over with Silver's leftover  pills that are in the medicine cabinet  because we know that the vast majority  of patients people that non-medical use  opioids they get them from a friend or  family for free and what that means is  that there were medications that were  prescribed that were left over we need  to do a better job with right sizing  those prescriptions and I think we're  starting to do that and my own Hospital  we actually just are speaking about  getting all the specialties that  prescribed talking about their most  common most frequent procedures and then  getting all the faculty on the same page  as far as what what the guidance will be  for how many pills should be prescribed  and the good thing about this is that  they often emphasized non opioids first  and the opioids only for the severe pain  if the other medications and other  modalities are not working despite all  the attention given to not prescribing  opioids and to reuse reduce our  utilization of opioids that's that they  clearly have a role in it in the  environment where I practice we often  see patients with severe chest pain  abdominal pain bad infections  particularly trauma fractures for  example so it's clear from some of these  problems that the patients can have  fractures severe trauma abdominal pains  even even cancer a chronic pain there is  certainly a role for opioids

